WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.330
A search for chargeback notes runs on an index split into five shards.

2
00:00:07.330 --> 00:00:13.017
Each shard indexes only its own documents, so any of them might hold the best matches.

3
00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.964
The coordinator, the node that took the request, needs the global top ten.

4
00:00:17.964 --> 00:00:22.600
The simplest plan: every shard sends back its ten best documents, whole.

5
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.540
That's fifty full documents to return ten.

6
00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:28.019
Forty were read, sent and thrown away.

7
00:00:28.233 --> 00:00:30.547
So the search runs in two phases.

8
00:00:30.547 --> 00:00:37.911
In the query phase, each shard scores its own matches and sends back only ids and scores for its top ten.

9
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.739
That's fifty small pairs.

10
00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:43.149
The coordinator merges them into the global top ten.

11
00:00:43.149 --> 00:00:46.952
Here, four come from shard two, and none from shard three.

12
00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:53.123
In the fetch phase, it asks for just those ten documents, and only from the shards that hold them.

13
00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:58.258
Now ask for page one thousand: skip 9,990 hits, return ten.

14
00:00:58.258 --> 00:01:04.897
No shard knows which of its hits get skipped, so each must find its top 10,000.

15
00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:12.872
The coordinator sorts 50,000 ids and scores to return ten documents, and the cost grows with every page.

16
00:01:13.067 --> 00:01:16.498
That's why OpenSearch stops at 10,000 by default.

17
00:01:16.498 --> 00:01:23.640
To page deeper, search_after passes the last hit's sort values, so each shard sends only its next ten.

18
00:01:23.833 --> 00:01:31.925
Ask every shard for ids and scores, fetch only the page you return, and page with search_after rather than skipping.
